---
import { BP4_SUPPORTING, PP3_SUPPORTING } from '../../src/constants.ts'
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro'
import { kindRows, oddsText } from '../lib/charts.ts'
import { gpcr, summary } from '../lib/data.ts'
import { percent } from '../lib/html.ts'
import { href } from '../lib/site.ts'

const s = summary()
const data = gpcr()
const chimeras = data.complexes.filter(c => c.gProteins.some(g => g.chimera)).length
const t = s.thresholds
const uncertain = s.alphaMissense.calls.uncertain
const HAN = 'https://research.nvidia.com/labs/dbr/assets/data/manuscripts/afdb.html'

const references = [
  ['Han, Tsenkov, Venanzi et al. 2026. AlphaFold Database expands to proteome-scale quaternary structures. Preprint.', HAN],
  ['Evans et al. 2021. Protein complex prediction with AlphaFold-Multimer. bioRxiv.', 'https://doi.org/10.1101/2021.10.04.463034'],
  ["Dunbrack 2025. Rēs ipSAE loquuntur: what's wrong with AlphaFold's ipTM score and how to fix it. bioRxiv.", 'https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.02.10.637595'],
  ['Varadi et al. 2024. AlphaFold Protein Structure Database in 2024. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkad1011'],
  ['Szklarczyk et al. 2023. The STRING database in 2023. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkac1000'],
  ['The UniProt Consortium 2025. UniProt: the Universal Protein Knowledgebase in 2025. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkae1010'],
  ['Landrum et al. 2018. ClinVar: improving access to variant interpretations and supporting evidence. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkx1153'],
  ['Cheng et al. 2023. Accurate proteome-wide missense variant effect prediction with AlphaMissense. Science.', 'https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adg7492'],
  ['Bergquist et al. 2025. Calibration of additional computational tools expands ClinGen recommendation options for variant classification with PP3/BP4 criteria. Genet Med.', 'https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gim.2025.101402'],
  ['Richards et al. 2015. Standards and guidelines for the interpretation of sequence variants. Genet Med.', 'https://doi.org/10.1038/gim.2015.30'],
  ['Nightingale et al. 2017. The Proteins API: accessing key integrated protein and genome information. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkx237'],
  ['Pollard et al. 2010. Detection of nonneutral substitution rates on mammalian phylogenies. Genome Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1101/gr.097857.109'],
  ['Dana et al. 2019. SIFTS: updated Structure Integration with Function, Taxonomy and Sequences resource. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gky1114'],
  ['Pándy-Szekeres et al. 2023. GPCRdb in 2023. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkac1013'],
  ['Mantel & Haenszel 1959. Statistical aspects of the analysis of data from retrospective studies of disease. J Natl Cancer Inst.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/jnci/22.4.719'],
  ['Sehnal et al. 2021. Mol* Viewer: modern web app for 3D visualization and analysis of large biomolecular structures. Nucleic Acids Res.', 'https://doi.org/10.1093/nar/gkab314'],
  ['Diesh et al. 2023. JBrowse 2: a modular genome browser with views of synteny and structural variation. Genome Biol.', 'https://doi.org/10.1186/s13059-023-02914-z'],
] as const
---

<Layout title="About" nav="about">
  <h1>About</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    An explorer for asking which partner a disease variant might disturb: predicted protein–protein interfaces and the ClinVar variants on them, in 3D and on the genome.
  </p>
  <h2>In short</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>{s.proteins.toLocaleString()} human disease proteins, with their AlphaFold DB heterodimer and homodimer predictions.</li>
    <li>Interface residues are matched to ClinVar missense variants, AlphaMissense scores and conservation.</li>
    <li>Pathogenic variants sit at predicted interfaces more often than benign ones, within the same protein.</li>
    <li>The complexes are predictions, so an interface variant is a lead, not a mechanism.</li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Method</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Proteins:</strong> all {s.proteins.toLocaleString()} reviewed human proteins with a UniProt disease annotation.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Complexes:</strong> heterodimers and homodimers from <a href={HAN}>AlphaFold DB's 2026 complex release</a>, kept at ipSAE ≥ {t.ipsae} in both directions. A homodimer is listed as a partner called "itself", and its interface is where one copy touches the other; AlphaFold DB models two copies, so for a larger assembly such as a tetramer it shows one of its interfaces.
      <details class="more">
        <summary>Details</summary>
        <p>
          Predicted with <a href="https://doi.org/10.1101/2021.10.04.463034">AlphaFold-Multimer</a> for STRING
          physical-interaction pairs. AlphaFold DB publishes only those it calls tentatively high-confidence:
          <a href="https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.02.10.637595">ipSAE</a> ≥ {t.ipsae} (the lower of chain A → B and B → A), mean pLDDT ≥ 70 and at most 10 backbone clashes.
        </p>
        <p>
          Where AlphaFold DB modelled the partner with another species' protein, the partner is named as the human protein of
          identical sequence. Where a partner was modelled twice, the more confident complex is kept.
        </p>
      </details>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Doubtful pairs</strong> are marked but still counted: {s.pairs.apart} "different compartments" and {s.pairs.paralogsWithoutStructure.toLocaleString()} "paralogs, no structure of both".
      <details class="more">
        <summary>Details</summary>
        <ul>
          <li>
            {s.pairs.apart} pairs join a protein UniProt places only inside mitochondria (outer membrane aside) with one it
            never places in mitochondria, such as the cytosolic and mitochondrial aspartyl-tRNA synthetases DARS1 and DARS2.
          </li>
          <li>
            {s.pairs.paralogs.toLocaleString()} pair a protein with a member of its own UniProt family. Paralogs do form real
            complexes, such as α- and β-tubulin, but AlphaFold can also model one protein's homodimer interface across two
            family members, so the {s.pairs.paralogsWithoutStructure.toLocaleString()} with no PDB entry holding both are
            marked.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </details>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Interface residues:</strong> any heavy atom within {t.contact} Å of the partner, residue pLDDT ≥ {t.plddt}, mapped to hg38 codons with EBI Proteins API coordinates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Variants:</strong> single-nucleotide missense variants from NCBI's weekly ClinVar (downloaded {s.provenance.clinvarDownloaded}).
      <details class="more">
        <summary>Details</summary>
        <p>
          Pathogenic, likely pathogenic, uncertain, likely benign or benign, from <code>variant_summary</code>, on a RefSeq
          transcript UniProt maps to the canonical isoform. Conflicting classifications are left out, and so is any variant
          whose wild-type residue does not match the canonical sequence at its position.
        </p>
      </details>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Pathogenicity:</strong> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1126/science.adg7492">AlphaMissense</a> scores {uncertain.scored.toLocaleString()} of the {s.variantsAtInterface.uncertain.toLocaleString()} interface variants of uncertain significance; {uncertain.pp3.toLocaleString()} ({percent(uncertain.pp3, uncertain.scored)}) reach supporting pathogenic evidence.
      <details class="more">
        <summary>Details</summary>
        <p>
          Scores come from the published table of every substitution in the canonical proteins. Read with the ClinGen
          calibration of <a href="https://doi.org/10.1016/j.gim.2025.101402">Bergquist et al. 2025</a>,
          {uncertain.pp3.toLocaleString()} ({percent(uncertain.pp3, uncertain.scored)}) have at least supporting evidence of
          pathogenicity (ACMG PP3, score ≥ {PP3_SUPPORTING}) and {uncertain.bp4.toLocaleString()}
          ({percent(uncertain.bp4, uncertain.scored)}) evidence of benignity (BP4, ≤ {BP4_SUPPORTING}). The developers'
          0.564 "likely pathogenic" cut-off does not reach supporting evidence, so the site does not use it.
        </p>
      </details>
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Conservation:</strong> mean phyloP over each codon, from UCSC's 100-way vertebrate alignment (multiz100way) bigWig.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Does the interface mean anything?</h2>
  <p>Within each protein, pathogenic missense variants sit at a predicted interface more often than benign ones:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>All residues: <Fragment set:html={oddsText(s.enrichment.all)} /></li>
    <li>
      Only folded residues (pLDDT ≥ 70): <Fragment set:html={oddsText(s.enrichment.ordered)} />. The buried core is rich in
      pathogenic variants and is not an interface.
    </li>
    <li>Only folded, surface-exposed residues: <Fragment set:html={oddsText(s.enrichment.orderedSurface)} /></li>
    {
      kindRows(s).map(({ label, e }) => (
        <li>
          {label}, folded surface only: <Fragment set:html={oddsText(e)} />
        </li>
      ))
    }
    <li>
      The same, comparing variants only within the same quarter of vertebrate conservation:
      <Fragment set:html={oddsText(s.conservation.enrichment.orderedSurface.matched)} />
    </li>
    {
      Object.entries(s.enrichment.byAlphaMissenseClass).map(([name, e]) => (
        <li>
          Only variants in the class "{name}": <Fragment set:html={oddsText(e)} />
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    Mantel–Haenszel odds ratios across proteins, with intervals from resampling proteins.
    The <a href={href('blog/2026-11-12-disease-variants-cluster/')}>findings post</a> draws these as charts, and
    <a href={href('blog/2026-11-12-are-the-interfaces-real/')}>another</a> checks the predicted interfaces against
    experimental structures and against random patches of the same protein's surface.
  </p>
  <details class="more">
    <summary>How to read these lines</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>The surface line rules out "folded vs disordered" as the explanation.</li>
      <li>
        The AlphaMissense lines show the interface adds little to a predictor that never sees the partner.
      </li>
      <li>The conservation line shows that conservation explains only part of it.</li>
      <li>Each confidence band of ipSAE, compared with residues at no interface, gives about the same odds ratio.</li>
    </ul>
  </details>
  <h2>Leads</h2>
  <p>
    The <a href={href('leads/')}>{s.leads.variants.toLocaleString()} leads</a> are the variants of uncertain significance at
    a confident interface residue where ClinVar classifies another missense variant pathogenic. Conditions are the names
    ClinVar's submitters gave, so one disease can appear under several names.
  </p>
  <h2>GPCRs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      {data.complexes.filter(c => c.dataset === 'DataSet1').length} experimental receptor–G protein complexes of biogenic
      amine receptors, from a GPCRdb selection made by BioHackathon Europe 2026 Project 9, plus {data.others.length} receptor
      structures without a G protein. The <a href={href('blog/2026-11-12-project-9-gpcrs/')}>GPCR post</a> shows what they find.
    </li>
    <li>Every receptor and Gα here is looked up in AlphaFold DB, whether or not it is a disease protein.</li>
  </ul>
  <details class="more">
    <summary>GPCR details</summary>
    <ul>
      <li>
        One olfactory receptor complex, OR51E2 with mini-Gs (8F76), is added to test AlphaFold DB's predicted Gαolf
        interfaces; <code>gpcr_structures.tsv</code> marks it as added.
      </li>
      <li>
        Contacts: heavy atoms within 5 Å between receptor and Gα. PDBe's SIFTS annotation gives each residue's UniProt
        position; a receptor chain SIFTS does not map is aligned to its UniProt sequence. Non-human proteins are aligned to
        the human ortholog. Receptor residues are labelled with GPCRdb generic numbers from GPCRdb's residue table for the
        human receptor, or their segment (e.g. ICL3) where it gives none.
      </li>
      <li>
        {chimeras} G proteins are engineered chimeras that SIFTS maps to no gene. Each stretch of such a chain is assigned
        to the human Gα it matches best, favouring the Gα the structure is annotated with.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </details>
  <h2>Isoforms</h2>
  <p>
    AlphaFold DB's monomer models of UniProt's alternative isoforms, for disease genes with at least one modelled besides
    the canonical. RefSeq transcripts come from UniProt's cross-references, which name the isoform each one encodes.
  </p>
  <details class="more">
    <summary>Isoform details</summary>
    <p>
      Each isoform's sequence is rebuilt from UniProt's alternative-sequence edits and checked against its model's sequence,
      which gives every isoform residue its canonical position. A replacement of the same length is compared residue by
      residue, so only residues that change count as lost.
    </p>
  </details>
  <h2>Caveats</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Coverage: {percent(s.withPartner, s.proteins)} of disease proteins have any predicted complex. AlphaFold DB modelled
      STRING physical-interaction pairs only; TP53 has none and KRAS has no RAF.
    </li>
    <li>
      The complexes are predictions. AlphaFold DB calls ipSAE 0.6–0.7 low confidence, 0.7–0.8 confident and ≥ 0.8 very
      high confidence, and the enrichment is about the same in each band.
    </li>
    <li>
      The enrichment itself is not new; the <a href={href('comparison/')}>comparison</a> lists who showed it first and what
      this explorer adds.
    </li>
    <li>An interface variant is a lead, not a mechanism: the residue may matter for folding or for another partner.</li>
    <li>
      Experimental interfaces from the PDB are shown in the
      <a href="https://genomes.jbrowse.org/protein-browser/">protein browser</a>; this explorer adds predicted ones.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>Future ideas</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Beyond human:</strong> the structural part (interface residues, surface patches, isoform comparison, the PDB
      check) works for any species with predicted complexes. The variant layers are human-only: ClinVar, AlphaMissense,
      the hg38 phyloP track and the genome links. Another organism needs its own variant source, such as OMIA for
      animals or curated mutant collections for yeast and fly, and conservation from a protein alignment where no
      genome-wide track exists. Mouse or yeast would be the natural second species, if AlphaFold DB models enough of
      their complexes.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <h2>References</h2>
  <details class="more">
    <summary>{references.length} references</summary>
    <ul class="references">
      {
        references.map(([text, url]) => (
          <li>
            {text} <a href={url}>{url.replace('https://', '')}</a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </details>
</Layout>
